  "answer_text": "Great question! It can feel a little awkward at first, but with the right approach, talking to someone with a lazy eye (amblyopia or strabismus) can be perfectly natural. Here are some helpful tips:\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Look at the Eye That Is Looking at You**\nLook at the eye which is looking at you! The person will notice if you are looking at the other eye, and even if you don't intend to be, it will feel to them as though you are staring at their \"wonky\" eye.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Remember \u2014 They Are Looking at You**\nWhat can throw you off is that it looks like one eye is looking at you and one is looking somewhere else. That can make it feel like they're not really making eye contact. Keep in mind that both their eyes are in fact looking at you \u2014 one of them just has a hard time staying centered.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Don't Pause or Act Confused**\nFor most people with a lazy eye, they feel like they're staring right at you. So don't pause and wonder if their attention is on you \u2014 just carry on with what you're saying.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Try Softening Your Gaze**\nBlurring your eyes slightly lets you look straight at them and listen better, without getting distracted by which eye to focus on. It's a simple trick that many people find helpful.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Don't Shift Your Gaze Awkwardly**\nMuch like anything unusual about a person, you will stop noticing it as you get to know them and other things about that person start to form your dominant image. Relax and give yourself permission to look at the non-dominant eye once in a while.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **If You Know Them Well, It's Okay to Acknowledge It**\nIf you can't concentrate, acknowledge it instead of avoiding eye contact. Even if they don't look at themselves all the time, they know it's distracting. Something like *\"Sometimes your eye starts moving and it throws me off\"* can lighten a possibly awkward situation. However, don't use this with a stranger or someone you've just met \u2014 just move on. When you're talking to someone you'll probably never see again, it's not worth making them feel self-conscious.\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Focus on the Nose Bridge as an Alternative**\nIf you truly can't figure out which eye to focus on, a helpful trick is to look at the bridge of their nose. It gives the appearance of eye contact without the confusion of picking one eye over the other.\n\n---\n\nThe most important thing is to **be natural and respectful**. People with a lazy eye are very aware of it and can tell when someone is uncomfortable, so staying relaxed goes a long way!",
